  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Idaho received $3.3B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The total represents 8,764 awards with an average award size of $379K. Obligations fell 15.5% year over year. Federal buying in Idaho has concentrated around a small set of agencies and vendors: the Department of Energy obligated $2.5B, which is the largest single agency source, and BATTELLE ENERGY ALLIANCE, LLC received $2B in obligations. Industry activity shows a strong tilt toward professional, scientific, and technical services, where All Other Professional, Scientific, and Technical Services accounted for $2B, and toward remediation work, where Remediation Services accounted for $533M. Aviation-related transportation and chemical manufacturing also appear among the top industries. The fiscal-year time series shows FY25 at $3.9B and FY26 at $3.3B, reflecting the current-year, fiscal year to date totals and the note that current-year totals are partial.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has federal contract spending in Idaho changed year over year?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 15.5% year over year. FY25 obligations were $3.9B and FY26 obligations are $3.3B to date. The five-year series shows FY21 at $2.8B, FY22 at $2.5B, FY23 at $3.2B, and FY24 at $3.8B, providing context for the current-year decline."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and vendors dominate Idaho federal contracting?",
        "body": "The Department of Energy is the top buyer, obligating $2.5B in FY2026 to date. BATTELLE ENERGY ALLIANCE, LLC is the top vendor with $2B in obligations, followed by IDAHO ENVIRONMENTAL COALITION LLC with $526M. Other notable agencies include the Department of Agriculture at $429M and the Department of Defense at $172M."
      }
    ]
  },
